  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="PARTIAL RECRYSTALLIZATION REGION">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">The partial recrystallization region is a phase where some grains have recrystallized while others have not, affecting the overall material characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="UNIQUENESS OF GRAIN STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Uniqueness of grain structure pertains to the distinct lattice arrangements within metal grains that significantly affect performance metrics.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="C STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Plain C steel is a type of steel used in various applications but exhibits sluggish recrystallization at low temperatures in the gamma region.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Temperature is a crucial parameter in materials science that significantly influences various processes and characteristics of materials. It plays an essential role in the kinetics of recrystallization and precipitation processes, which are fundamental to the manipulation and enhancement of material properties. Specifically, temperature affects phase transformations and the mechanical properties of steel, particularly during operations like rolling and heating, where precise control of temperature is necessary to achieve desired material attributes.

Moreover, temperature is vital in regulating the recrystallization and grain growth processes in metals and alloys. These processes are critical for determining the final structure and performance of the material. The control of temperature during these stages can lead to improved mechanical properties, such as strength and ductility, thereby enhancing the overall reliability and functionality of metallic components in various applications. 

In summary, temperature is an integral factor in the evolution of materials, influencing both their physical transformations and mechanical behaviors, and it is particularly important in the context of metals and alloys during processes like recrystallization, grain growth, and shaping through rolling and heating.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="LOW-TEMPERATURE TOUGHNESS">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Low-temperature toughness is an essential mechanical property of materials that signifies their ability to absorb energy and undergo plastic deformation when subjected to low temperatures. This property is critical in determining the performance and reliability of materials in cold environments, where they are subjected to various stress conditions. Low-temperature toughness indicates how well a material can resist fracture under these conditions, making it a vital consideration in applications such as construction, manufacturing, and engineering. Understanding and measuring low-temperature toughness help engineers select appropriate materials for specific applications, particularly those exposed to severe cold or fluctuating temperature conditions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="HOLDING TIME">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Holding time in the context of heat treatment affects the grain size and properties of steel during processes like recrystallization.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="SOLUTE NB">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Solute Nb influences the incubation time for the start of recrystallization and affects the delayed recrystallization process when alloyed with steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="FINE NB(C,N)">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">Fine Nb(C,N) precipitates that occur during the deformation process, notably influencing the kinetics of recrystallization in Nb-modified steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="TIME CONSTANT">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The time constant is a parameter in Avrami's law that describes the time required for a fraction of a material to undergo recrystallization.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="GRAIN REFINEMENT">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Grain refinement is a fundamental process primarily aimed at reducing the size of grains within the microstructure of materials, particularly steel. This strategic reduction of grain size is significant as it enhances the mechanical properties of the final product. 

The primary objective of grain refinement is to produce finer grains that can substantially improve a material's strength, toughness, and ductility. By minimizing grain size, the process contributes to superior mechanical characteristics, as smaller grains can impede dislocation movement, leading to greater resistance to deformation.

In summary, grain refinement plays a crucial role in the metallurgical landscape, particularly in steel production, by effectively reducing grain size to bolster mechanical performance. This enhancement is essential for various applications where superior material properties are critical.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">4</data>
  <data key="d4">8</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="AVRAMI'S LAW">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Avrami's Law mathematically describes the fraction of material that has undergone a transformation over time, often used in phase transitions such as recrystallization.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="OHMORI">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Ohmori is a researcher who suggested the retarding effect due to fine precipitation in the context of recrystallization and deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="LE BON ET AL.">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Le Bon et al. provided insights into the recrystallization kinetics of Nb-modified steel and contributed to the understanding of precipitation processes.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="NON-RECRYSTALLIZATION REGION">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">The "non-recrystallization region" in the steel rolling process refers to a specific temperature range where the steel material undergoes deformation without undergoing recrystallization. This region significantly influences the final microstructure and mechanical properties of the steel being processed. During this phase of deformation, the absence of recrystallization indicates a limitation to the grain refinement that can be achieved. The non-recrystallization region is critical because it determines how the steel will behave under stress and affects its overall material characteristics.

In essence, the non-recrystallization region plays a pivotal role in defining the mechanical properties of steel. When steel is deformed within this temperature range, the material retains its microstructural characteristics from the previous processing stages without the formation of new grains. This lack of recrystallization can lead to a variety of outcomes in the material's performance, as the steel may exhibit enhanced hardness or strength but reduced ductility compared to fully recrystallized materials.

Understanding the non-recrystallization region is essential for metallurgists and engineers, as it helps in predicting the behavior of steel during processing and in tailoring the material properties to meet specific requirements for various applications. The effective management of temperature and deformation conditions within this region is crucial for optimizing the quality of the final product.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">5</data>
  <data key="d4">5</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="DENSITY">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Density is a physical property that is affected by the temperature during deformation processes, particularly in the non-recrystallization region.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="GRAIN-BOUNDARY AREA">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Grain-boundary area refers to the total surface area at the boundary between grains, which can affect material strength and behavior under deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="GAMMA-GRAIN">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Gamma-grains are a specific type of microstructural feature that plays a critical role in the mechanical properties of steel, especially when affected by deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="ALPHA-GRAIN">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Alpha-grains refer to the ferritic structure that forms during the transformation of austenite, affecting the material's overall mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="ASTM GRAIN-SIZE NUMBER">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">ASTM grain-size number is a quantitative measure that indicates the average size of grains in a material, significant for assessing material properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="ROLLED MATERIAL">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Rolled material refers to metal that has been shaped and altered through the rolling process, often affecting its microstructural properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">3</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSFORMATION RATE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Transformation rate is the speed at which the phase change occurs in materials during processing, significantly affecting overall material attributes.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">21</data>
</node>
<node id="NUCLEATION RATE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Nucleation rate is the rate at which new grains form during the phase transformation, influencing microstructural evolution.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">21</data>
</node>
<node id="INTERFACE NUCLEATION GROWTH MECHANISM">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">The interface nucleation growth mechanism describes how new grain boundaries are formed and grow during phase transformations in materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">21</data>
</node>
<node id="TWO-PHASE REGION">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">The two-phase region is a critical temperature range in which two distinct phases, specifically austenite and ferrite, coexist within steel materials. This region significantly influences the mechanical properties and microstructure of the steel during various deformation processes. The presence of both phases in the two-phase region alters the behavior of the material, impacting its strength, ductility, and overall performance.

This simultaneous presence of austenite and ferrite creates unique structural conditions that are essential for optimizing the processing and performance of steel. As the temperature varies within this region, the proportions of the phases can change, which in turn affects the mechanical properties of the material. Understanding the two-phase region is vital for material scientists and engineers in order to control and improve the properties of steel during production and application.

In summary, the two-phase region is defined by the coexistence of austenite and ferrite phases at specific temperatures, influencing the mechanical characteristics and microstructure of steel during processing.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">4</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="MICROSTRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Microstructure pertains to the small-scale structural characteristics of materials, particularly emphasizing its critical role in assessing the mechanical properties of alloys. This concept encompasses the microscopic arrangement and organization of various components within a material, influencing both its mechanical properties and its behavior during various processes. Understanding microstructure is essential for predicting how materials will perform under different conditions, making it a vital consideration in materials science and engineering. As such, microstructure serves as a key determinant of a material's overall performance and reliability.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">7</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="YIELD STRENGTH">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Yield strength is defined as the stress level at which a material begins to undergo plastic deformation, transitioning from elastic behavior to permanent deformation. This property is crucial for evaluating the performance of materials, especially in structural applications. When the yield strength is reached, the material will no longer return to its original shape upon the removal of the applied stress, indicating that it has entered a state of permanent deformation. Thus, understanding yield strength is essential for assessing the structural integrity of materials, as it provides insight into how they will behave under various loads and stresses. Overall, yield strength is a key mechanical property that plays a vital role in material selection and engineering design.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">11</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="TENSILE STRENGTH">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Tensile strength refers to the maximum amount of tensile (stretching) stress that a material can withstand before experiencing failure. This property is particularly significant in the context of steel alloys, where it serves as a critical measure of performance and durability. Understanding the tensile strength of materials is vital for engineers and designers, as it helps in selecting appropriate materials for various applications, ensuring that structures and components can endure the stresses they will face in use. Thus, tensile strength is an essential characteristic that influences the design and integrity of various structures and products made from steel and other materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSITION TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Transition temperature refers to the temperature at which transformations occur in steel, affecting its properties and microstructure during processing.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">5</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="ABSORBED ENERGY">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Absorbed energy is the energy a material can absorb before fracturing, often measured in impact tests.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="EQUIAXED ALPHA GRAINS">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Equiaxed alpha grains refer to a specific grain structure that is roughly equal in all dimensions, which contributes to improved material properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="STAIN-INDUCED TRANSFORMATION">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Strain-induced transformation is a phenomenon where the material undergoes a phase change due to applied mechanical strain.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="AUSTENITE-FERRITE REGION">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">The austenite-ferrite region refers to a specific temperature range in steels where both austenite and ferrite phases coexist and interact during processing.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="NB(C, N)">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Nb(C, N) refers to niobium carbide and niobium nitride, which are precipitates that can improve the mechanical properties of steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="LIMITING VALUE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Limiting value indicates the maximum extent of grain refinement achievable through deformation in specified conditions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="OPTICAL MICROSCOPIC SCALE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The optical microscopic scale refers to measurements and observations of microstructures that can be captured using optical microscopy techniques.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">17</data>
</node>
<node id="ELECTRON MICROSCOPIC SCALE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Electron microscopic scale involves more detailed examinations of microstructures using electron microscopy, allowing for higher resolution insights.)("relationship"</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">17</data>
</node>
<node id="DILATOMETRIC METHODS">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Dilatometric methods are used to measure changes in dimension of materials as a function of temperature, typically employed to study thermal expansion and phase transitions in metals and alloys.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="STRAIN-INDUCED AR3 TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The strain-induced Ar3 temperature refers to the temperature at which austenite transforms into ferrite during the cooling process under deformation, which is critical in the heat treatment of steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="MARTENSITE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Martensite is a hard and brittle microstructure that forms in steel during the rapid cooling of austenite, a phase of iron and steel that is typically stable at high temperatures. This transformation occurs through a process known as quenching, where the temperature of the austenite is quickly lowered, leading to a significant increase in the material’s strength and hardness.

The formation of martensite is critical in metallurgy, particularly in the heat treatment of steel, as it enhances the mechanical properties of the material. The inherent brittleness of martensite, while it contributes to increased hardness, can also present challenges in applications where ductility is necessary. Nonetheless, the unique characteristics of martensite make it essential in various engineering applications where high strength and wear resistance are required. 

In summary, martensite is a vital microstructure achieved through rapid cooling of austenite, characterized by its high hardness and strength, yet accompanied by brittleness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">5</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="POLYGONAL FERRITE-PEARLITE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Polygonal ferrite-pearlite is a microstructure found in steel consisting of alternating layers of ferrite and cementite, typically resulting in a balance of strength and ductility.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="ACICULAR FERRITE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Acicular ferrite is a specialized form of ferrite noted for its unique, fine, needle-like microstructure, which significantly improves the toughness and strength of steel, especially in applications involving welded structures. This microstructure is primarily formed during controlled rolling processes, where it develops at lower temperatures, thus contributing to the enhanced mechanical properties of steel.

Characteristically, acicular ferrite is composed of fine, non-equiaxed ferrite along with dispersed cementite and islands of martensite. This specific configuration enables the material to exhibit remarkable toughness and strength, making it highly desirable in the production of high-performance steel used across various industries. The unique arrangement of the microstructural components not only enhances the resistance to brittle fracture but also improves durability under stress, which is critical in structural applications. 

Overall, the presence of acicular ferrite is a key factor in advancing the performance qualities of steel, especially in environments that demand reliable mechanical integrity and resistance to deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">8</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="TEXTURE DEVELOPMENT">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Texture development refers to the evolution of crystallographic orientation in materials, significantly affecting their mechanical and physical properties during processing like rolling or forging.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">12</data>
</node>
<node id="TEXTURE INTENSITY">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Texture intensity quantifies the degree of preferred orientation of grains in a polycrystalline material, affecting its anisotropic mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">12</data>
</node>
<node id="YUTORI AND OGAWA">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Yutori and Ogawa are researchers who studied the effects of processing conditions on the microstructure and properties of controlled-rolled steels.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="TANAKA AND TABATA">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Tanaka and Tabata are researchers who reported on the determination of Ar3 temperature in steels based on texture intensity measurements and controlled-rolling conditions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSFORMED MICROSTRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Transformed microstructure refers to the microstructural changes that occur during heating and cooling of steel, and is critical in determining the final mechanical properties of the material.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="HEAVY DEFORMATION">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Heavy deformation refers to significant plastic deformation applied to materials to improve their mechanical characteristics and to alter their microstructure, such as during processes like rolling or forging.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC TEXTURE">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Crystallographic texture describes the preferred orientation of grains in a polycrystalline material, which greatly influences its mechanical properties and response to processing.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">12</data>
</node>
<node id="ROLLING DIRECTION (RD)">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Rolling direction is the orientation of a material as it undergoes rolling processes, significantly affecting the texture and mechanical properties achieved in the final product.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">12</data>
</node>
<node id="NORMALIZED STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Normalized steel refers to steel that has been heat-treated to refine its microstructure to achieve a more uniform quality and improve its mechanical performance.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="QUENCHED AND TEMPERED STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Quenched and tempered steel is steel that has undergone hardening by quenching followed by tempering, enhancing its strength and toughness for various applications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="CONTROLLED-ROLLED STEEL">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Controlled-rolled steel is a specialized type of steel characterized by its unique mechanical properties, which are significantly influenced by its rolling process. This type of steel is produced through specific rolling practices designed to enhance certain attributes, such as grain size and toughness. 

One important aspect of controlled-rolled steel is its anisotropic nature, meaning that its properties can vary depending on the direction of the applied force. This characteristic is crucial for applications where directional strength and durability are required. Additionally, controlled-rolled steel tends to exhibit embrittlement in specific directions, necessitating careful consideration during its use in engineering and manufacturing processes.

The careful management of the controlled rolling conditions leads to a refined microstructure within the steel, enhancing its overall performance and making it suitable for various demanding applications. Through controlled rolling, manufacturers can tailor the mechanical properties of the steel to meet specific requirements, making it a preferred choice for industries that require high-performance materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">17</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="PLANE ANISOTROPY">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Plane anisotropy is a concept that denotes the directional dependency of mechanical properties in materials, particularly in relation to the microstructural development observed in rolled steel. This phenomenon plays a crucial role in determining how materials respond under various loads and conditions, significantly influencing their strength and fracture behavior.

In the context of rolled steel, plane anisotropy highlights the variations in mechanical properties that occur when measured along different directions within the material. This directional dependence arises from the inherent microstructural characteristics established during the rolling process, which aligns the material’s grain structure in a specific orientation. As a result, steel exhibits different mechanical strengths, ductility, and fracture toughness based on the test direction relative to the rolled sheet.

Understanding plane anisotropy is essential for engineers and materials scientists when designing components that require specific mechanical performance attributes. It is vital for applications where directional loading occurs, ensuring that materials are utilized in a manner that optimizes their performance and reduces the likelihood of failure. 

Overall, plane anisotropy is a key factor in the mechanical analysis of rolled steel, impacting its overall reliability and efficiency in practical applications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="EMBRITTLEMENT">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Embrittlement is a reduction in ductility, causing a material to fracture easier, particularly influenced by the formation of certain crystal textures.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="TEXTURE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Texture in materials science describes the orientation distribution of grains within a material, significantly affecting its mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="CHARPY IMPACT ENERGY">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Charpy impact energy measures a material's toughness and is evaluated using standardized specimen tests to assess energy absorption during fracture.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">4</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="LONGITUDINAL DIRECTION">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The longitudinal direction in materials refers to the direction of rolling or lengthwise orientation, influencing material properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="THROUGH-THICKNESS DIRECTION">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The through-thickness direction refers to the orientation perpendicular to the plane of a material sample, critical in evaluating properties such as strength and embrittlement.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="TRANSVERSE DIRECTION">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The transverse direction is perpendicular to the rolling direction and affects the mechanical properties of materials in distinct ways.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="CHARPY CURVES">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Charpy curves represent the relationship between impact energy and temperature in materials, showing how toughness varies.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="SEPARATIONS">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Separations refer to defects observed in materials, particularly during mechanical testing. These defects signify a weak structural integrity, which is critical for evaluating the performance and reliability of materials under various conditions. The occurrence of separations is notably linked to the development of cracks in steel, which can arise due to anisotropy in fracture strength. Anisotropy refers to the directional dependence of a material's mechanical properties, meaning that its behavior can vary based on the orientation of the applied stress.

In conditions where the material is subjected to stress, separations can lead to reduced toughness and increased brittleness. This phenomenon is especially relevant in steel, where the uneven distribution of strength in different orientations can result in significant performance issues. Consequently, understanding and addressing separations is essential for ensuring the structural integrity and durability of materials, particularly in applications where mechanical strength is crucial. Overall, separations highlight the importance of assessing material properties and behaviors to prevent failures in engineering and manufacturing processes.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">9</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="CLEAVAGE FRACTURE">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Cleavage fracture is a brittle fracture that can occur in materials, particularly metals, under certain stress conditions, often influenced by microstructural characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="MNS INCLUSION">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Manganese sulfide (MnS) inclusion is an impurity found in steel that can trigger crack initiation and propagation, affecting the material's mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="UNIAXIAL YIELD STRESS">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Uniaxial yield stress is the stress required to deform a material plastically in a uniaxial stress state, a critical parameter in material performance.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="TEMPER EMBRITTLEMENT">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Temper embrittlement is a phenomenon in metals where brittleness is increased due to segregation of impurities at grain boundaries during heat treatment.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">18</data>
</node>
<node id="GRAIN BOUNDARY">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Grain boundaries are interfaces between individual crystals or grains in a material, significantly influencing its mechanical and physical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">18</data>
</node>
<node id="RARE EARTH METALS">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Rare earth metals are a collective group of 17 chemical elements known for their significant role in enhancing the properties of various materials, particularly steels. These elements contribute to modifying the shape of inclusions within steel, which is critical for improving its overall performance. In addition to their application in steel production, rare earth metals are utilized in small quantities across various alloys and materials to impart desired characteristics, such as increased toughness and enhanced resistance to oxidation. Their unique chemical properties make them essential in numerous industrial applications, underscoring their importance in modern materials science.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="DESULPHURIZATION METHOD">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Desulphurization methods are processes designed to reduce sulfur content in steel, aiming to prevent embrittlement from sulfide inclusions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="DIMENSIONAL ANISOTROPY">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Dimensional anisotropy is a property of materials where mechanical properties vary depending on the direction of measurement, often observed in rolled steels.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="CRACK INITIATION">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Crack initiation is the process whereby a crack begins to form in a material, often influenced by stress concentrations and microstructural features.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="HOTPROLLED STRIP">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Hot-rolled strip is a product of steel processing involving rolling at high temperatures, which affects its microstructure and mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="PLANE STRAIN">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Plane strain is a condition in which deformation occurs primarily in a plane, significant in understanding yielding and failure in materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="PLANE STRESS">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Plane stress is a condition where the stress in the thickness direction of a material is negligible, affecting the analysis of stress and strains during manufacturing.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="PROPAGATE">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Propagate refers to the propagation of cracks or defects in materials, a critical aspect of understanding failure mechanisms in engineering materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">9</data>
</node>
<node id="CONTROLLED-ROLLED STEELS">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Controlled-rolled steels are materials processed under specific temperature and deformation conditions to achieve desirable mechanical properties such as increased strength and toughness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="SULPHIDE">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Sulphides are impurities in steels that can negatively impact mechanical properties, and controlling their shape is crucial for enhancing the final product quality.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="MARTENSITIC STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Martensitic structure in steel indicates a specific microstructure formed by rapid cooling, which results in increased hardness and strength.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="DUCTILE-TO-BRITTLE TRANSITION TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The ductile-to-brittle transition temperature is a critical thermal parameter that plays a significant role in materials science, particularly in evaluating the performance of metals and alloys under varying temperature conditions. This temperature serves as a threshold, indicating the specific temperature range within which a material transitions from ductile behavior, characterized by the ability to deform plastically, to brittle behavior, where it exhibits brittle fracture.

When the temperature falls below the ductile-to-brittle transition temperature, materials, particularly steels, are prone to sudden and catastrophic failure without significant prior deformation. This transition is especially relevant in cold conditions where the ductility of steel can be drastically reduced. Understanding and determining this temperature is essential for predicting how materials will behave under different environmental conditions, thus influencing engineering and design decisions in various applications, particularly in construction and aerospace industries.

Overall, the ductile-to-brittle transition temperature is a crucial factor that affects the integrity and reliability of materials subjected to low temperatures. Proper knowledge of this parameter ensures that engineers can select suitable materials that will maintain their mechanical integrity and performance under expected operating conditions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="EFFECTIVE GRAIN SIZE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Effective grain size is a key concept in material science that refers to the size of grains within a material that significantly impacts its mechanical properties during processing. This measurement is particularly important in understanding the behavior of materials, especially those containing martensitic phases. In these materials, the effective grain size plays a crucial role in determining their strength, ductility, and overall performance. By focusing on the grains that have the most influence on the material's properties, researchers and engineers can better predict how materials will behave under various conditions, ultimately leading to the design of more efficient and effective materials for a range of applications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="SEPARATION">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Separation refers to the phenomenon where internal flaws or microstructural changes propagate under stress, impacting the integrity of materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">5</data>
</node>
<node id="BAND DENSITY">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Band density is a measure of the number of bands (or clusters of grains) within a microstructure, which influences material properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">5</data>
</node>
<node id="CR">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Chromium is an alloying element that helps to improve the hardness and corrosion resistance of steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="PRECIPITATION HARDENING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Precipitation hardening is a specialized heat treatment process utilized in metallurgy to enhance the strength and hardness of materials. This process involves the formation of fine, dispersed particles within the metallic matrix of the material. These particles serve to impede the movement of dislocations, which are defects in the crystal structure that can lead to material deformation under stress. By creating these obstacles to dislocation motion, precipitation hardening significantly increases the material's mechanical properties, making it more durable and resistant to deformation.

Overall, precipitation hardening is crucial in developing high-strength alloys and materials used in various applications, including aerospace, automotive, and structural components, where improved mechanical performance is essential.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="LATTICE HARDENING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Lattice hardening refers to the strengthening of materials through alterations in the lattice structure that impede the movement of dislocations.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="SOLID-SOLUTION HARDENING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Solid-solution hardening is achieved by adding solute atoms to a metal, which distorts the crystal lattice and makes it harder for dislocations to move.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="TEXTURE HARDENING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Texture hardening occurs when the crystallographic texture of a material is altered, affecting its mechanical properties and nucleation of cracks.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="HESLOP AND PETCH">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Heslop and Petch are researchers who demonstrated the relationship between grain size and ductile-to-brittle transition temperature.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="T_{RS}">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">T_{rs} represents the transition temperature as a function of various metallurgical parameters and compositions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">10</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="KUNITAKE">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Kunitake is a researcher who contributed to the understanding of transition temperatures in martensitic structures.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="GLADMAN">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">Gladman is a researcher who established the transition temperature equations for bainitic steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="FINISHING TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Finishing temperature is the temperature at which steel is processed, significantly impacting its mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="DISLOCATIONS">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Dislocations are defects in the crystal structure of materials that affect their mechanical strength and ductility.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="Σ_{D}">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">σ_{D} is a parameter representing Ashby-Orowan strengthening effects on the yield strength of materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="N_{B}(C, N)">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Nb(C, N) refers to niobium carbonitride precipitates that influence dislocation and recovery mechanisms in Nb steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="C-MN STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">C-MN STEEL is a type of low-carbon low alloy steel distinguished by its favorable mechanical properties, which are largely a result of its phase structure. This category of steel exhibits excellent workability and toughness, making it a favored choice in various industrial applications. One of the key attributes of C-MN STEEL includes its unique characteristics in dislocation hardening and recovery processes, which contribute to its strength and resilience under stress. Overall, C-MN STEEL combines both versatility and performance, making it suitable for a range of engineering and construction projects.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">7</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="SUBGRAINS">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Subgrains are smaller grains formed from the division of larger grains in a material and influence its mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="BAINITIC STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Bainitic steel is a type of steel with a specific microstructure that provides a balance between strength and ductility.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="HIGH-ANGLE BOUNDARY">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">High-angle boundaries are types of grain boundaries that can impede crack propagation, enhancing toughness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="LOW-ANGLE BOUNDARY">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Low-angle boundaries are less effective at obstructing dislocation movement compared to high-angle boundaries, affecting the ductility of steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="V">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Vanadium (V) is an alloying element that contributes significantly to strength and transition temperature via precipitation hardening in steel.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="TI">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Titanium (Ti) is an alloying element that helps to refine grain size and can affect the recrystallization process in steel, although large additions can decrease toughness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">2</data>
</node>
<node id="STRAIN-INDUCED PRECIPITATES">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Strain-induced precipitates are fine particles formed in steel during deformation that can inhibit grain growth and enhance strength without degrading toughness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="SI">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Silicon (Si) is an alloying element that improves strength and ductility in controlled-rolled steel, particularly when used in amounts of 0.3–0.5%.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="MANGANESE">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Manganese (Mn) is a crucial alloying element in structural steel, known for its significant role in enhancing material strength through processes such as solid-solution hardening and grain refinement. It achieves this by lowering the transformation temperature, which is vital for improving the mechanical properties of steel. The incorporation of manganese not only contributes to the overall strength of the alloy but also plays a key role in increasing its durability and resistance to wear. As a result, manganese is indispensable in the production of high-performance structural steel, making it essential for various engineering applications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="ALPHA-MATRIX">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">The alpha-matrix is a phase of steel characterized by its body-centered cubic (BCC) structure, playing a crucial role in the mechanical properties and toughness of the material.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="GAMMA-RECRYSTALLIZATION">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">Gamma-recrystallization refers to the process where the gamma phase in steel transitions to other phases during heating and deformation, affecting the overall strength and structure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="MOLYBDENUM">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Molybdenum is a crucial alloying element used predominantly in the steel industry to enhance the mechanical properties of steel. It plays a significant role in increasing the strength of steel primarily through a process known as transformation hardening. This process contributes to the development of acicular ferrite steel, a type of steel renowned for its high strength and toughness. 

In addition to its role in strength enhancement, molybdenum also improves the hardness and corrosion resistance of steel, making it a vital component in the production of various high-performance steel grades. Its effectiveness in fortifying steel makes it indispensable in applications where durability and reliability are critical, such as construction, automotive, and heavy machinery industries. 

Overall, molybdenum's unique properties as an alloying element help to create superior steel materials that can withstand extreme conditions and stress, thereby extending the lifespan and functionality of steel products.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="ACICULAR FERRITE STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Acicular ferrite steel is characterized by its unique microstructure that enhances strength, influenced by the addition of molybdenum.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="PLATE MILL">
  <data key="d0">EQUIPMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">The plate mill is a type of rolling mill that has challenges with reduction per pass, especially during initial slab rolling.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="HOT-STRIP MILL">
  <data key="d0">EQUIPMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">A hot-strip mill can achieve significant reduction per pass, producing finer and more uniform grain structures than a plate mill.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="CHROMIUM">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Chromium suppresses the formation of a banded structure in steel, preventing separations and aiding in the formation of fine precipitates when used with Nb or V.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="COPPER">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Copper has a negligible effect on properties in controlled-rolled steel but can be effective in combination with other elements like Nb and V.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="NICKEL">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Nickel (Ni) is a significant metallurgical element primarily recognized for its role as an alloying agent that enhances the properties of steel. It is particularly valued for improving the toughness and strength of steel products. In the context of controlled-rolled steel production, nickel's concentration is typically limited to 0.3% or less. At this lower percentage, nickel has a minimal impact on the steel's overall properties when produced. Instead, its effectiveness is often observed when combined with other elements such as niobium (Nb) and vanadium (V), which work synergistically to improve the material characteristics of the steel. Thus, while nickel contributes positively, its role is more pronounced in the presence of these other alloying elements.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="VANADIUM">
  <data key="d0">ALLOYING ELEMENT</data>
  <data key="d1">Vanadium is a crucial alloying element extensively utilized in steel production, recognized for its significant contributions to enhancing the material's hardness and strength, especially in hot-rolled applications. Through the alloying process, vanadium plays a vital role in grain refinement, which is essential for achieving improved mechanical properties in steel products. This is accomplished through the formation of fine precipitates, which help reinforce the steel matrix, thereby elevating the overall strength and durability of the steel. The incorporation of vanadium not only helps in achieving desired performance characteristics but also allows for the production of high-strength steels that are essential in various industrial applications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="PASS SCHEDULE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The pass schedule is a process parameter in rolling mills that defines the sequence of operations for reducing material thickness and achieving desired microstructure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="ROLLING TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Rolling temperature is a crucial process parameter in steel manufacturing, influencing the microstructure and mechanical properties of the final product.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="LOW-CARBON STEEL">
  <data key="d0">STEEL GRADE</data>
  <data key="d1">Low-carbon steel is defined as a type of steel that comprises a carbon content of less than or equal to 0.06%. This low percentage of carbon imparts distinct properties, particularly ductility, which allows it to be shaped and formed easily, making it suitable for a wide range of applications. Due to its characteristics, low-carbon steel is widely utilized in various steel processing methods, demonstrating its importance in numerous industrial and commercial sectors. Overall, low-carbon steel is valued for its versatility and favorable mechanical properties, making it an essential material in the field of steel manufacturing and application.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">8</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="FINISH ROLLING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Finish rolling is the final stage of the rolling process that shapes the metal piece into its final dimensions and properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="CEMENTITE">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">Cementite, or iron carbide, is a hard material found in steel that affects its mechanical properties, particularly toughness and hardness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="POLYGONAL FERRITE">
  <data key="d0">PHASE</data>
  <data key="d1">Polygonal Ferrite is a phase observed in steel that can influence the material's overall mechanical properties depending on its volume fraction in the microstructure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="DUAL-PHASE STEEL">
  <data key="d0">MATERIAL</data>
  <data key="d1">Dual-Phase Steel refers to a type of steel that has a microstructure consisting of α-grains and martensite islands, characterized by specific yield and tensile strength properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="FINISH-ROLLING TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Finish-Rolling Temperature refers to the specific temperature at which steel is rolled to affect its microstructure and alter its mechanical properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">1</data>
</node>
<node id="WORK HARDENING">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Work Hardening refers to the process where a material increases in hardness and strength through plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="FINE-GRAINED POLYGONAL FERRITE">
  <data key="d0">MICROSTRUCTURE</data>
  <data key="d1">Fine-grained polygonal ferrite refers to a microstructure which consists of very small ferrite grains resulting from controlled deformation processes, enhancing strength and toughness.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="STRENGTH">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Strength is a critical mechanical property of materials that quantifies their ability to withstand an applied force or load without failure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="TOUGHNESS">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Toughness is a significant mechanical property that characterizes a material's ability to absorb energy while undergoing plastic deformation without fracturing. This property is essential in various steel applications, as it ensures that the material can endure stress and impact without breaking. Toughness allows materials, particularly metals, to maintain structural integrity under dynamic loads, making it vital for manufacturing processes and end-use scenarios where durability and resilience are critical.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">4</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="NORMALIZING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Normalizing is a heat treatment process that involves heating steel to a temperature where it becomes austenitic, followed by cooling in air to refine its microstructure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="QUENCHING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Quenching is a rapid cooling process used in metallurgy to harden steel by transforming austenite into martensite.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="TEMPERING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Tempering is a heat treatment process applied to quenched steel to reduce brittleness and increase toughness by allowing some of the martensite to transform.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="DEFORMATION BANDS">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Deformation bands are structural features that emerge during the plastic deformation of materials. These bands play a significant role in influencing the nucleation of new phases as materials undergo cooling processes. Their formation occurs under various mechanical processes, including rolling, where they affect the microstructural properties and overall mechanical performance of the material.

Specifically in materials like steel, deformation bands contribute to mechanical properties by separating adjacent grains or subgrains, which can lead to changes in strength and ductility. This ability to alter microstructural conditions highlights the importance of deformation bands in materials science, as they significantly impact the performance characteristics of metals subjected to mechanical stress.

Overall, deformation bands serve as critical elements that shape the structural integrity and properties of materials, particularly during and after processes of plastic deformation.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="SUPRAPOSITION">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Supraposition effects in thermomechanical treatments refer to the combined influences of different processes (like controlled rolling and transformation) to optimize material properties.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="CHARPY TEST">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">The Charpy test is a standardized high-strain-rate test to determine the amount of energy absorbed by a material during fracture.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="EFFECT OF FINISHING TEMPERATURE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The effect of finishing temperature on steel properties impacts yield strength and transition temperature, indicating how thermal processes influence material performance.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="LOW-TEMPERATURE RANGE">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">The low-temperature range refers to the temperature conditions under which controlled rolling enhances the strength and toughness of steel by refining its microstructure.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="HEAVY, REPEATED DEFORMATIONS">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Heavy, repeated deformations in steel processing are used to achieve grain refinement, contributing to increased strength and toughness of steel products.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">13</data>
</node>
<node id="THROUGH-THICKNESS EMBRITTLEMENT">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Through-thickness embrittlement occurs in rolled steels, particularly affecting toughness, where weakened structural integrity leads to failure in certain directions.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="STRAIN-INDUCED PRECIPITATION">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">Strain-induced precipitation is a significant phenomenon in materials science that occurs when new phases develop within the microstructure of a material as a result of applied stress. This process typically takes place during deformation and involves the formation of precipitates, which are secondary phases that emerge within the matrix of the material. 

The formation of these precipitates plays a crucial role in enhancing the mechanical properties of the material. Specifically, they contribute to strength improvements by obstructing the movement of dislocations. Dislocations are defects within the crystal structure that facilitate plastic deformation; thus, by hindering their motion, strain-induced precipitation can effectively increase the hardness and strength of the material.

In summary, strain-induced precipitation represents a critical interaction between applied stress and material microstructure, leading to the emergence of new phases that enhance mechanical performance by obstructing dislocation motion. This intricate relationship underscores the importance of understanding microstructural changes in the development of stronger materials.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="SOLUTE-DRAG EFFECT">
  <data key="d0">PHENOMENON</data>
  <data key="d1">The solute-drag effect refers to the resistance to dislocation motion caused by solute atoms, which can strengthen the material during deformation processes.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="DISLOCATION">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Dislocations are defects within the crystal structure of materials that play a significant role in plastic deformation and the overall strength of metals.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="PROCESS VARIABLES">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS PARAMETER</data>
  <data key="d1">Process variables are the various factors in processing—such as temperature, strain rate, and composition—that impact the final properties of the material.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="COOLING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Cooling is the process applied after deformation, such as in quenching, to achieve desired microstructures like martensite in steels.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">1</data>
  <data key="d5">0</data>
</node>
<node id="I. KOZASU">
  <data key="d0">PERSON</data>
  <data key="d1">I. Kozasu is a researcher recognized for his contributions to the field of metallurgy, particularly in understanding the properties of steel. He has co-authored a number of papers that explore the characteristics and behaviors of steel and its alloys, which have been published in various prestigious metallurgical journals. His work includes a significant focus on alloy microstructures, underscoring his expertise in the intricate relationships between material composition and performance. Through his scholarly efforts, I. Kozasu has established himself as a notable figure in the scientific community, contributing valuable insights that advance knowledge in metallurgy and enhance the application of steel in various industries.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">4</data>
  <data key="d5">20</data>
</node>
