<node id="CONTROLLED ROLLING OF STEEL">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">This is a process used to refine the properties of steel by regulating temperature and deformation during manufacturing to achieve desired mechanical characteristics.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">1</data>
  <data key="d4">2</data>
  <data key="d5">4</data>
</node>
<node id="MICROALLOYING">
  <data key="d0">PROCESS</data>
  <data key="d1">Microalloying is a metallurgical technology focused on enhancing the properties of steel through the addition of small amounts of alloying elements. This process involves the careful integration of these microalloying elements, which leads to significant improvements in the mechanical characteristics of steel without substantial changes to its overall composition. By employing microalloying techniques, metallurgists can achieve desired performance attributes such as increased strength, improved toughness, and enhanced ductility. These enhancements contribute to the development of high-performance steel grades suitable for various industrial applications, further underscoring the importance of microalloying in the field of metallurgy.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">4</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="DUCTILITY">
  <data key="d0">MECHANICAL PROPERTY</data>
  <data key="d1">Ductility is a key mechanical property of materials, particularly significant in the context of steel manufacturing and engineering. It refers to the ability of a material to undergo significant deformation before rupture when subjected to tensile stress. This property is essential for understanding how materials behave under load and is a critical factor in assessing various grades of steel, including low-carbon steel, which is known for its enhanced ductility.

The measurement of ductility helps in evaluating the performance and suitability of materials in structural applications. In steel manufacturing, ductility ensures that products can withstand bending, stretching, and other forms of mechanical stress without failing. Therefore, materials with higher ductility, like certain steel grades, are preferred in applications requiring resilience and flexibility, as they can absorb energy and deform without breaking.

Overall, ductility is an important characteristic that informs the design and selection of materials in engineering, emphasizing the need for materials that can tolerate deformation under stress while maintaining structural integrity.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">2</data>
  <data key="d4">3</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
<node id="AMERICAN INSTITUTE OF MINING, METALLURGICAL AND PETROLEUM ENGINEERS">
  <data key="d0">ORGANIZATION</data>
  <data key="d1">The American Institute of Mining, Metallurgical, and Petroleum Engineers (AIME) is a prominent professional association located in New York, specializing in the fields of mining, metallurgy, and petroleum engineering. The organization plays a pivotal role in advancing these industries by organizing conferences that facilitate knowledge sharing and networking among professionals. AIME is also dedicated to the dissemination of research by publishing scholarly works pertaining to mining and metallurgical engineering, with a notable focus on steel and metallurgy.

As a recognized entity in these engineering domains, AIME contributes significantly to the professional development of its members and the broader community by providing access to key research findings and innovations. Through its various initiatives, the institute aims to support the growth and sustainability of mining, metallurgical, and petroleum engineering practices, ensuring that members stay at the forefront of industry developments.

Overall, the American Institute of Mining, Metallurgical, and Petroleum Engineers stands as a crucial institution for professionals within its fields, fostering collaboration and promoting advancements through its conferences and publications.</data>
  <data key="d2">tanaka1981</data>
  <data key="d3">3</data>
  <data key="d4">5</data>
  <data key="d5">14</data>
</node>
