</edge>
<edge source="T. TANAKA" target="S. MATSUNO">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">S. Matsuno and T. Tanaka contributed jointly to research documented in Tetsu-to-Hagané</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GRAIN STRUCTURE" target="FERRITE">
  <data key="d6">5.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The presence of ferrite influences the overall grain structure and thus impacts the mechanical behaviors of steel.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GRAIN STRUCTURE" target="DEFORMATION">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Deformation during the controlled rolling process significantly impacts the grain structure and mechanical properties of steel.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="FERRITE">
  <data key="d6">29.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Austenite and ferrite are two crucial phases in the structure of steel that significantly influence the material's properties through their interactions during the processes of heating and cooling. These phases are particularly important in the context of controlled rolling processes, where precise temperature management dictates the transformations that occur in the steel.

Austenite, which is stable at high temperatures, can undergo a transformation into ferrite when the material is cooled. This transformation is marked by the Ar3 temperature, which serves as a critical transition point where austenite begins to convert into ferrite under specific conditions. The cooling phases of steel play an essential role in this conversion, highlighting the importance of temperature control in achieving desired steel characteristics.

The transformation of austenite into ferrite is fundamental in steel processing, as it affects not only the strength and ductility of the final material but also its overall performance in various applications. Understanding the dynamics between austenite and ferrite during heating and cooling is crucial for metallurgists and engineers aiming to optimize steel properties for specific uses. 

In summary, the interplay between the austenite and ferrite phases during the cooling of steel is critical in determining the material's overall properties and performance. The transformation process, particularly during controlled rolling, underscores the significance of temperature management in steel production.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="ALPHA">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Austenite transforms into alpha during the phase transformation in steel when cooled or deformed, which is crucial for determining the material's properties.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="UPPER BAINITE">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Austenite can also transform into upper bainite during certain cooling conditions, influencing the microstructure and mechanical properties of the steel.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="MIXED GRAIN STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The formation of a mixed grain structure is attributed to the behavior of gamma-grains during processing</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="MARTENSITE">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Austenite transforms into martensite during rapid cooling, which is a key aspect of controlled rolling and heat treatment processes in steel</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="BAINITE">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Austenite can also transform into bainite at certain cooling rates, affecting the mechanical properties of steel</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AUSTENITE" target="NORMALIZING">
