  <data key="d7">Nb enhances the strength and toughness of C-Mn steel through grain refinement and precipitation hardening.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HALL-PETCH RELATION" target="YIELD STRENGTH">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The Hall-Petch relation describes how the yield strength of polycrystalline solids increases as grain size decreases.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="COARSE Γ" target="BAINITE">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Coarse γ transforms into a mixture of coarse α and bainite, which negatively impacts notch toughness and overall mechanical properties.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BAINITE" target="LOW-TEMPERATURE TOUGHNESS">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Low-temperature toughness is a mechanical property affected by the presence of bainite in the microstructure</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BAINITE" target="MARTENSITE">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Both bainite and martensite are microstructural phases resulting from the transformation of austenite under different cooling conditions, influencing the toughness and strength of the steel</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BAINITE" target="POLYGONAL FERRITE-PEARLITE">
  <data key="d6">5.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Polygonal ferrite-pearlite can be part of the microstructural classification of steels, contrasting with bainitic structures which form under different cooling scenarios</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GAMMA" target="ALPHA">
  <data key="d6">17.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The entities involved are "GAMMA" and "ALPHA," which refer to phases in the context of steel processing. GAMMA, also known as the austenite phase, plays a crucial role in influencing the microstructural characteristics and mechanical performance of steel. During processes such as recrystallization and deformation, the GAMMA phase undergoes transformation to the ALPHA phase, also called ferrite, as temperatures decrease and under specific deformation conditions. This transformation is critical to steel processing, as the transition from GAMMA to ALPHA affects the final properties of the material, including its strength, ductility, and overall performance. Therefore, both phases are integral to understanding the behavior of steel during heating, cooling, and mechanical working, with the GAMMA phase initially providing a critical high-temperature structure that is essential for the formation of the lower-temperature ALPHA phase through controlled processing techniques.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GAMMA" target="WARM-WORKED">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Warm-worked structures involve the gamma phase and indicate the characteristics that result from deformation at specific temperatures, influencing the transformation to other microstructures.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GAMMA" target="DEFORMATION BAND">
  <data key="d6">5.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Gamma grains can be divided by deformation bands, significantly influencing the nucleation of alpha.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ALPHA" target="RECRYSTALIZATION">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The recrystallization process is crucial for the formation of fine-grained alpha in steel, as it helps to refine the gamma phase before deformation occurs.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ALPHA" target="CLASSIC HOT ROLLING">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">In classic hot rolling, alpha nucleation is limited to gamma-grain boundaries, affecting grain refinement.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ALPHA" target="MEDIUM HARDENABILITY">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Medium hardenability is significantly influenced by the transformation processes involving the alpha phase in steel.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ALPHA" target="HARDENABILITY">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The hardenability of steel is largely determined by the presence of the alpha phase, which affects its toughness and strength.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FINER GRAIN STRUCTURE" target="MIXED GRAIN STRUCTURE">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Achieving a finer grain structure is critical to avoiding a mixed grain structure, which may lead to inconsistencies in mechanical performance.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FINER GRAIN STRUCTURE" target="TOTAL REDUCTION">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">An increase in total reduction during deformation is directly related to achieving a finer grain structure in metals, thereby enhancing mechanical properties.</data>
