<edge source="MOLYBDENUM" target="LOW-CARBON STEEL">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Molybdenum is often added as an alloying element to low-carbon steels to improve strength and toughness</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VANADIUM" target="LOW-CARBON STEEL">
  <data key="d6">7.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Vanadium is included in low-carbon steel to enhance strength, particularly after hot-rolling</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LOW-CARBON STEEL" target="MICROALLOYING">
  <data key="d6">16.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Microalloying is a process extensively utilized to improve the mechanical properties of low-carbon steel. This technique involves the addition of small amounts of alloying elements, such as niobium, vanadium, and titanium, to the steel during its production. The primary aim of microalloying is to refine the grain structure and increase the strength, toughness, and overall performance of low-carbon steel. As a result, low-carbon steels treated through microalloying exhibit enhanced characteristics that make them suitable for a variety of demanding applications in industries like construction, automotive, and manufacturing. The synergy between microalloying and low-carbon steel demonstrates a significant advancement in metallurgy, leading to the development of materials with superior properties compared to conventional low-carbon steels.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LOW-CARBON STEEL" target="DUCTILITY">
  <data key="d6">9.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Low-carbon steel is known for its high ductility, making it suitable for various applications requiring considerable deformation</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LOW-CARBON STEEL" target="TOUGHNESS">
  <data key="d6">8.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Low-carbon steel exhibits good toughness, an advantageous characteristic for many structural applications</data>
</edge>
<edge source="POLYGONAL FERRITE" target="STRENGTH">
  <data key="d6">6.0</data>
  <data key="d7">The formation of polygonal ferrite within the microstructure is intrinsically related to the overall strength of the material, affecting how it performs under stress.</data>
</edge>
<edge source="STRENGTH" target="TOUGHNESS">
  <data key="d6">1.0</data>
  <data key="d7">Strength and toughness are interrelated mechanical properties that define how a material behaves under load; the balance between them is crucial for many applications.</data>
</edge>
